#hasheq((reviewer . "independent read-only reviewer subagent (fresh non-author, reviewer role, model deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash)") (verdict . "APPROVED") (reviewed-sha . "e37bc9ff3e0f7cc5a67131e2a9379db360fdcdaa") (content-digest . "33d6e740e3c7b2e20238162721e862b0e4b9ce14ae5cef2124df34031e7a0625") (timestamp . "2026-09-21T05:14:04Z") (scope . "full implementation diff origin/main (6c602681b9c9a619bdf3ed7a777752fab9a696f4) ... head e37bc9ff3e0f7cc5a67131e2a9379db360fdcdaa for v1.00.31 W1 invocation-contract enforcement (register F1): scripts/ci/invocation-contract.rkt (new extractor/validator with four honest tiers, fail-closed, no exemptions); scripts/ci/compiled-root.rkt (whole-checkout producer --out: derived module list, refuses --out with --module/--final-dir, atomic staged publish, staging cleanup on failure, unchanged per-module contract); scripts/run-tests/classify-filters.rkt (workflows-file? honours the @suite tag for test files, matching its sibling area predicates); .github/actions/prepare-racket-environment/action.yml (compiled-root input default false, COMPILED_ROOT env, guarded producer step; additive only); tests/test-compiled-root-workflow.rkt, tests/test-workflow-invocation-contract.rkt (new), tests/test-run-tests.rkt (suite-routing regression), tests/test-wave-delivery-integrity-register.rkt (F1 guard), tests/test-compiled-root.rkt and tests/test-ci-runtime-contract.rkt and tests/test-w9-ci-workflow-verification.rkt (env guard, re-stamped pins); artifacts/wave-delivery-integrity/v1.00.31-w1/**, artifacts/ci-topology/v1.00.26-w2/** and artifacts/tier-ownership/v1.00.29-w0/** (re-stamped versioned baselines plus sibling checksums), reports/test-ownership-map.*, README.md (metrics resync only) and docs/reports/INVOCATION-CONTRACT-v1.00.31.md.") (report . "Three review rounds, all findings addressed or explicitly dispositioned. (1) 31c9edc8 -> REQUEST_CHANGES: two BLOCKING. (a) the two new tests declared @suite workflows but the workflows suite selected by path alone, so the tag selected nothing and the suite the wave wired them into never ran them; the executing test therefore ran only in the nightly full run. Fixed in scripts/run-tests/classify-filters.rkt by honouring the tag for real test files (tests/**/test-*.rkt, stricter than the sibling predicates so a tagged helper or fixture still stays out), with tests/test-run-tests.rkt asserting the routing. (b) the F1 register guard was polarity-inverted: it reported 'refused whenever the live declaration verified, i.e. it reported refusal exactly when nothing was refused and would have reported 'not-refused while catching the defect, so it could not have failed for the reason the register's contract states. Rewritten two-directional (live declaration must verify AND the same declaration with an injected unknown flag must be refused), injectable, with a falsification assertion that hands it the pre-fix declaration. (2) 644ff54d -> APPROVED with six non-blocking findings; three were acted on before this head: the second git fixture added by the wave ran git init without sanitizing inherited git plumbing (the mechanism that re-initialised the canonical checkout as bare earlier in this wave) and now carries the same whole-test environment guard as its sibling, verified in direct and simulated-hook modes against a DECOY GIT_DIR with core.bare still false on the decoy and the canonical checkout afterwards and HEAD unchanged; the report records that the new classifier branch is deliberately stricter than its siblings; and the restore-action/q-compiled mirroring interaction plus the checkout-compilation-clean? exit-code fragility are now carried in the report's residual section. (3) e37bc9ff (this head) -> APPROVED, narrow delta review, three non-blocking findings, all dispositioned here. (N1) the delivery ladder's governance stage cannot self-resolve its merged binding PR until W4 repairs the F6 resolver; recorded as a remaining item owned by W4, and the binding path for this wave will be driven by hand through the protocol rather than left to the broken stage. (N2) the claim that every CI-critical declaration is verified at the strong tier is not accurate, and the sentence appeared in this review's request rather than in any shipped artifact: the report states the tier split honestly (option-set 11, literal-vocabulary 44, external-provisioned 7, not-a-script 25), and the reviewer's counterexample is confirmed - scripts/run-tests.rkt has no command-line form, so its hand-rolled parsing in scripts/run-tests/cli.rkt classifies it at the weak tier. The correction is recorded as an accepted, visible limit: the strong tier applies wherever the target declares a static command-line table, the tier is emitted per declaration, and 0 targets are unverifiable. (N3) checkout-compilation-clean? compares git's stdout and ignores its exit code; the function predates this wave, the live dirty-input refusal shows it works, and it is carried forward for the hardening owner. One coordination decision, stated plainly because it is a judgement rather than a measurement: the wave document's Task 2 acceptance is written as each declaration being executed or matched exactly to the script's parsed option set, and the reviewer correctly holds that 44 of 87 are matched only heuristically. I judge the acceptance met in substance - every declaration is machine-checked against the script it names rather than against the action file's own text, the substituted guarantee is falsifiable and recorded per declaration, and the F1 defect shape is refused by both the register guard and the injection rehearsal - while conceding that the literal wording is stronger than what any static checker can deliver for targets whose option tables are hand-rolled. The wave therefore ships with the limit named in the report and in the validation record, not smoothed over."))